<main id="main" class="wrap project-page"><a class="back-link" href="../index.html#projects">All projects</a><p class="eyebrow">Multimodal learning · Visual reasoning</p><h1>Scene Graphs for Visual Question Answering</h1><p class="lead">Generating structured relationships between objects to improve visual question answering when scene graph annotations are missing.</p><div class="detail-result"><strong>11%</strong><span>GraphVQA accuracy improvement on scene graph-deficient test sets</span></div><div class="prose"><p>This multimodal machine learning class project explored how scene graphs—structured representations of objects and their relationships—can support visual question answering (VQA).</p>
<h2>My contribution</h2><p>I designed a scene graph generation pipeline using MotifNet and bottom-up attention networks, then fine-tuned GraphVQA with generated scene graphs. This improved accuracy by 11% on scene graph-deficient test sets.</p>
<h2>Research directions</h2><ul><li><strong>Fine-grained reasoning:</strong> use object-level and relationship information alongside global visual features to help answer questions about a scene.</li><li><strong>Pre-training:</strong> explore masking relationships and object features so models learn to recover missing contextual information.</li><li><strong>Low-resource datasets:</strong> investigate scene graph features for prompt initialization and generate scene graphs where annotations are unavailable.</li></ul>
